## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks (calendar pass).** This course's checks sit in week 17 (mid-year) and week 32 (year-end,
   finals), off every grade 9 core check week. The CALENDAR.md table shows ELD grades 9 to 12's grade 9 mid-year
   check in week 17 too; grades 9 to 12 have no stagger rule but the finals week (`frame.py calendar` checks K to 8
   only), so the pick is to keep it and name it in the pass.
6. **A stale note this frame set off.** `stale.py check` now fails
   docs/courses/career-pathways-grades-9-to-12/frame.md line 172, whose Pending marker waited on this frame; its
   writer or the lead rewrites that note to say what is now true (not edited here: another course's frame).
2. **Picks that change two row notes** (listed under Row changes in the final report): the history case is satellite
   positioning, not the mail system or a map tool (a map route tool is grade 8's emerging-technology pick, g8-emerging-design),
   and the emerging technology is quantum computing set against classical computing, not a map by a person against a
   route tool (the same repeat). Grade 7's history picks (the telephone exchange, the personal computer, a large
   database) are not repeated.
3. **Simulated heights.** g9-simulate-data's note says "heights with a stated spread"; a spread beyond a range
   suggests a bell-shaped distribution the math progression has not taught by grade 9 (lesson 13). Pick: the note
   reads "values in a stated range". Listed under Row changes.
4. **Our own editor for grade 9.** The course needs the editor's Python text view with a step-through trace, stated
   tests, built-in random and math libraries and support for one small class (STANDARD.md "Our own editor"), plus
   three toy pages (operating system, simulated device, security) and a queue simulation page, all for TOOL-ASKS.md
   when the scope is written. The paper path meets every id except the three device-only ones in full, so the course
   does not wait on them.
5. **Source list.** docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md: NIST SP 1270 (2022), gps.gov's history pages and the NIST post-quantum
   cryptography project, to be opened and dated by the unit writers (no fetch was made for this frame).
# Course frame: Computer Science, Grade 8

Course `computer-science-grade-8` (catalogue id `cs-k12`), grade 8, one year, the last of the three middle school courses. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, after the kindergarten pilot; the scope and sequence sets the days later.

## The course in brief

- **Units.** 5, each a concept area at its grade 8 depth, in the order the ideas build: systems that hold up
  (systems and security, so the year's toy security page, device rules and accessibility tests come first), make it,
  credit it, hand it over (programming, the year's text project), data that answers back (data and analysis, after
  math grade 8 teaches scatter plots), AI under test (algorithms and machine learning, using unit 3's data and unit 2's
  project), and new technology, our town (computing and society, the year's closer).
- **Days.** 64 course days: 60 in units, 2 for course-level checks (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 5) and 2 respond days
  (one after each check). Two lessons a week of about 45 minutes, 32 weeks: the computer science default for grades 6
  to 12 (docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md section 2, Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1), an elective, so course days 2k-1
  and 2k fall in week k. The 45 minutes hold set-up, clean-up and collecting work: a lesson opens with a five-minute
  retrieval trace, a wrong example from the last exit ticket, or a short concrete moment (APPROACH.md, "How a day
  opens"), and device lessons keep three minutes at each end to get devices out, saved and put away, so a device
  lesson teaches for about 34 minutes and a paper lesson for about 40. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit
  assessment and end with a respond day, with one shape for a unit that a course-level check follows directly (the
  kindergarten pilot's pattern): in units 2 and 5 the check stands for the unit's own end-of-unit assessment and the
  check's respond day is the unit's respond day, so no week holds an assessment and a respond day just before a check.
  Units 1 to 5 run 13, 15, 12, 12 and 8 days. Days follow the load of the rows: unit 2 carries the year's multi-week
  text project (g8-text-project, "a multi-week text project") with the attribution and refine-for-users rows inside it,
  the heaviest load, and gets 15; unit 1 has four systems rows, two of them design-and-test rows, and gets 13; units 3
  and 4 own three and four ids with device days and get 12 each; unit 5's four society rows are single unplugged days
  in the progression, and with a two-lesson debate get 8. 64 lessons for 18 rows (13 owned ids) is about three and a
  half a row, against the kindergarten pilot's two, because grade 8 rows carry a device version, a written product or a
  project. No unit runs over 25 days, so there are no mid-unit assessments.
- **Check weeks.** Grade 8 checks are kept off core check days (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, stagger rule 3, grades 6 to 8
  by school day; the grade 8 core check school days are civics 48, 49, 96, 97, 145 and 146, ELA 89, 90, 125 and 126,
  math 70 and 116, science 75, and week 32's finals days). The mid-year check is course day 29, school day 73, week 15
  (after unit 2), with its respond day on course day 30, school day 75 (a respond day, not a check day). The year-end
  check is course day 63, school day 158, week 32, the finals week (stagger rule 3), with its respond day on course day
  64, school day 160. Unit 5 runs in weeks 28 to 31 before it. Overlaps with other after-core subjects' check days are
  the lead's calendar pass across the after-core frames (For the lead 1).
- **Standard.** The 13 MS ids the progression gives grade 8 to own, of the 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards:
  MS-SYS-SE-33, MS-SYS-IM-36, MS-PRO-PD-14, MS-PRO-TR-20, MS-DAT-DI-25, MS-DAT-DI-27, MS-DAT-IM-29, MS-ALG-ML-08,
  MS-ALG-IM-11, MS-ALG-PS-05, MS-PRO-RD-18, MS-SOC-ET-41, MS-SOC-ET-42 (STANDARD.md "Ownership of MS and HS ids", 13 in
  grade 8). Grade 8 is the last middle school owner, so every MS id is owned by the end of this year. Five more rows use
  seven ids owned in grades 6 and 7 in a new setting (~MS-SYS-NT-35, ~MS-SYS-HW-30, ~MS-ALG-PS-01, ~MS-PRO-RD-17,
  ~MS-SOC-HI-39, ~MS-SOC-HU-43, ~MS-SOC-CE-44); they are named in "What students learn" as used again, never in Owned
  standards. Each owned id is taught at the depth its owner row's notes set, in our own words (STANDARD.md "Depth"; the
  boundary statements are never copied or quoted), and "What students learn" says what is out. Plans cite ids and state
  each action in a short phrase of our own (STANDARD.md "Quoting"); the credit line "Standards: 2026 CSTA PK-12
  Computer Science Standards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)" goes on any day that leans on a statement. Check set: each unit plan
  checks itself against the computer science bar (docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md part 5, items 1 to 9) and the lens
  (docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md), and reads the partial base unit it adapts for order; the "Check set adds"
  column names what the writer expects that to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. No Specialty id, and
  no X + CS id, is claimed. Lists go past the middle school boundary (g8-text-project notes, Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
  q-fix-cs-2): the list work in unit 2 is about 4 days (days 15 and 16 and parts of the build days), about 6 percent of
  the 64, inside bar criterion C1's 10 percent.
- **Practices and concept areas.** The Units table's fifth column (the C3 column of the core frames) names, for
  computer science, the concept area each unit teaches and the CSTA practices it teaches inside the content, by code
  and our label (STANDARD.md section 3), as bar items 1 and 2 ask. Each unit plan names the practice's day and task.
  Across the year: CT6 define the problem (units 1, 3), CT7 abstract and find patterns (units 2, 3), CT8 build (units
  2, 4), CT9 test, debug and refine (units 1, 2, 4), IC3 explain and give feedback (units 2, 3, 4, 5), IC4 run a
  project (unit 2), IC5 be a reliable teammate (units 1, 2, 5, pair and team roles), HCD10 include different users
  (units 1, 5), HCD11 prototype and test for access (units 1, 2), HCD12 keep users informed (unit 4, the model card),
  ESR1 weigh who gains and who is harmed (units 3, 4, 5), ESR2 credit others' work and protect users' data (units 1,
  2). Dispositions are named in the unit plans, not here.
- **Base text.** Partial: England's open computing curriculum, the Teach Computing Curriculum, archived edition
  (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived, key stage 3, resources dated 2023), which its publisher no longer maintains;
  England's current lessons are on Oak National Academy (as the kindergarten frame records). England's Year 9 is the
  grade 8 age. License: the scout read the Open Government Licence v3.0 "unless expressly stated otherwise" on the
  publisher's terms page; the archive's unit pages carry no license line of their own, so each file's license line is
  read when the store folder is made. Adapted unit by unit and rephrased for an American audience (STANDARD.md section
  2, Cory 2026-11-16): US spelling, school years named as US grades, no money in pounds and no English law, no product
  or company name, our own editor and pages in place of named apps. Three of its six Year 9 units feed units 1, 2 and 3 (two lessons of the Python unit, the rest of which grade 7 adapts), with one Year 8 lesson the grade 7 frame offered;
  its animation, audiovisual representation and physical computing units are left out (see Source map). Units 4 and 5
  are our own: the key stage 3 curriculum has no AI unit and little of the society strand (docs/reviews/
  computer-science-open-material-2026-11-16.md). The source's name appears only in a day's credit line, once a day.
  Pending(docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md#teach-computing-curriculum): the source store holds no copy of this base, so the
  Source map rests on the public unit pages (lesson titles and unit overviews, opened 2026-11-25), and each adapted
  lesson's check for third-party images, text and data waits on the store folder (For the lead 2).
- **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") defines them, in computer science's words (APPROACH.md):
  Understanding is an unplugged or trace lesson, the first teaching of a construct or idea (a network map cut and
  traced, a worked twenty-line program traced with a trace-table, a dataset followed through its stages on paper).
  Fluency is a modify lesson: change a working program, rule or chart in small checked steps (unit 2 days 16 and 17,
  unit 4 day 48, and the five-minute retrieval traces). Application is a make, design or society lesson: design and
  build a program against criteria, test a system with users' stated needs, run a data investigation, write a
  justified verdict or debate with sources (unit 1 days 2, 5, 9 to 11, unit 2 days 18 to 23 and 25 to 27, unit 3 days 34, 35, 37,
  38 and 40, unit 4 days 46 and 52, unit 5 days 56 and 58 to 60). Check is the end-of-unit assessment or a course-level
  check; Respond acts on it by error letter. Inside a unit: understanding before modify before make, and no make day
  before a trace or modify day on the same construct.
- **Evidence.** Daily: an exit ticket with a short program or case and "what happens?" or "who does this change?",
  answered in writing with wrong answers by error letter; on modify and make days the program itself against two or
  three look-fors and one line on what was tested and changed (APPROACH.md, "The form of daily evidence"). Scored: every
  end-of-unit assessment is a sitting with unseen items, never the unit's own anchor, of about 35 minutes on paper or
  device. Two units also score a product whose draft, written feedback and revision days are counted in the unit:
  unit 2's text project (draft day 23, written feedback days 24 and 25, revision days 26 and 27, hand-in day 28, on a
  rubric) and unit 3's investigation summary (draft day 37, written feedback and revision day 38). Each owned id has
  its own look-for: a "modify" or "refine" id (MS-ALG-IM-11, MS-PRO-TR-20) by a changed rule or program retested, a
  "justify", "summarize" or "evaluate" id (MS-PRO-PD-14, MS-DAT-DI-27, MS-ALG-ML-08, MS-SOC-ET-41) by the student's
  own written reasoning on a new case, a "debate" id (MS-SOC-ET-42) by a written claim with its backing and a rebuttal,
  never by recognition of a supplied answer. Units 2 and 5 are assessed in the course-level check that follows them
  (Days). No score comes from a lesson that first teaches a row. The respond day reteaches by error letter and repeats
  the missed item in a new form.
- **Devices.** One device per pair is the default on a device day, with roles (driver and navigator) that switch at a
  stated time and every student typing in every device lesson. Device days: unit 1 days 4 and 8 (a toy inbox and a
  practice sign-up page built into our pages), unit 2 days 15 to 17, 19, 20, 22, 23 and 25 to 28 (our editor, Python
  text), unit 3 days 32, 33, 34 and 35 (a table-and-scatter tool), unit 4 days 44, 45, 48, 51 and 52 (a classifier test
  page, our editor), and the year-end check's code items where devices allow. Every unit keeps at least one unplugged
  day (APPROACH.md, Pacing). Every device day is written four ways (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"): one device per
  pair, a few devices at stations with the other pairs on the paper version, one screen with a student at the device
  for each step, or no device. Owned codes that need technology: MS-DAT-DI-25 is met only on a device (STANDARD.md
  list); on the no-device path the student plots the points by hand and draws the trend line, and the course record
  marks it "part met without a device". MS-ALG-PS-05 is met only on the teacher's one screen with a tool the school has
  already approved and no student accounts; a school with none uses Curio Class's saved outputs and marks the id "part
  met" (STANDARD.md "Generated-text tools", Cory, Desk q-prework-cs-1). Every other owned code is met in full on paper:
  printed test results and a printed model card (MS-ALG-ML-08), printed code samples run by hand on a paper test-table
  (MS-PRO-RD-18), a paper prototype of a program's screens tried by two classmates against the stated needs
  (MS-PRO-TR-20), a printed sign-up page with its barrier (MS-SYS-IM-36).
- **Paper path.** Every lesson has a paper version: programs written and traced on paper and checked by a partner who
  is "the computer", trace-tables, list-diagrams, printed tables plotted by hand, printed samples and cards. No tool
  asks for a student account, stores a student's name or sends anything off the device (STANDARD.md "Privacy and
  safety"); security lessons use toy systems built into the page, with no scanning and no attack on any real system.
- **Reading level and vocabulary.** Code read on a page is at most twenty lines (APPROACH.md, Supports). Each unit names
  five to eight words taught in depth, with words that have an everyday meaning taught as two meanings (bug, cloud,
  model, license, feature). Sentence frames: "I predict ___ because line ___ says ___" and "This decision changes the
  experience for ___ because ___". Error messages in our tools are plain words we write. A student may answer a trace
  in numbers, arrows or the home language, and each key says which wrong answers can come from reading the words.
- **Swap or leave out.** STANDARD.md section 3 lets a school swap or leave out a named unit for its community's
  reasons. Unit 5 may be swapped for other emerging technology cases and another local debate topic, or left out:
  MS-SOC-ET-41 and MS-SOC-ET-42 are then not claimed that year and the family note names them; the course still meets
  computing and society through unit 4's harms and bias rows and unit 2's attribution row. Unit 3 may be swapped for
  other supplied data sets, not left out, so the course always claims MS-DAT-DI-25, MS-DAT-DI-27 and MS-DAT-IM-29 (bar
  part 5 fails a course with nothing on data). Units 1, 2 and 4 carry the year's security, programming and AI ids and
  the project the later units use, and are not swapped or left out.
- **Safety.** No lab or field work, so no laboratory or field safety guide applies. Device lessons keep cords behind
  the table and devices carried closed with two hands; security lessons run only on toy systems inside our pages,
  never on a real school or home system or account. The scope and sequence copies these lines into each device and
  security day.
- **Costs.** Provisional, for a class of 30, beyond the school's own devices: unit 1 $4 (printing network maps, attack
  and prevention cards, the printed sign-up page and user-need cards), unit 2 $5 (printing the worked game program,
  trace-tables, test-tables, the credit-case cards and the project rubric), unit 3 $5 (printed tables, graph paper for
  the hand plots, the stage cards), unit 4 $4 (printed test results, model cards and code samples), unit 5 $4 (case
  cards and two debate source packets), about $22 in all; no consumables and no board.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
  no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Unit 1 has disability content (users who work by keyboard alone, by
  screen reader or with enlarged text) and online safety content (fake messages asking for a password). Before it
  reads, the agent studies a starting pack: the W3C Web Accessibility Initiative's "How People with Disabilities Use the
  Web" (w3.org/WAI/people-use-web/), the Autistic Self Advocacy Network's "Identity-First Language" page
  (autisticadvocacy.org/about-asan/identity-first-language/), one community's own account, not taken as every
  community's, and the National Center on Disability and Journalism's Disability Language Style Guide
  (ncdj.org/style-guide/), and widens it to the brief's five sources, two of them owned by disabled people's own
  organizations, and records them. It checks that no task asks a student to act out a disability (needs are test
  conditions, not roles) and that the online safety days carry health's "If a student tells you something" steps word
  for word (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md; APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground). Unit 5 touches rural communities, older adults,
  families with low incomes and speakers of languages other than English (the video visit case): the agent studies
  the federal report itself (the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation's 2022 report on
  telehealth use in 2021, aspe.hhs.gov), the National Telecommunications and Information Administration's Internet
  Use Survey (ntia.gov) and the National Digital Inclusion Alliance's own pages (digitalinclusion.org), and adds a
  source from a rural or language community's own organization; it checks that the talk stays on how the service was
  designed, never on a classmate's home, health or devices. Unit 5's career day
  and unit 4's lottery case can touch families' work and means (APPROACH.md, "Work and automation"): the agent checks
  no task asks about a family member's job, money or transport. Unit 3's datasets and unit 4's classifier hold no data
  about real or identifiable people. The course's checks get the answer-key agent's read. No unit is hands-on in the
  laboratory sense, so no safety agent read is planned beyond the Safety lines above, which the sensitivity read
  confirms are on each day.

