1: # Course frame: Computer Science, Grade 9
2: 
3: Course `computer-science-grade-9` (catalogue id `cs-k12`), grade 9, one year. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, written on the shape of the kindergarten pilot (docs/courses/computer-science-kindergarten/frame.md and its fix report); the scope and sequence sets the days later.
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 6, every concept area in the year, in the order the ideas build: lists and records (programming and
8:   algorithms, the structures every later program uses, then designing with them), cleaner programs (procedures,
9:   loops and modules, which rewrite unit 1's programs), algorithms that decide (deterministic and probabilistic
10:   algorithms, a first tour of AI algorithm types and a first audit, using unit 1's lists and unit 2's library),
11:   data you can trust (data dictionaries and simulated data, reusing unit 3's random choices), what the system does
12:   for us (operating systems, device limits, a network read and security measures) and computing over time
13:   (a technology's history, an emerging technology against an established one, and a private career note).
14: - **Days.** 64 course days: 60 in units, 2 for course-level checks (1 after unit 3, 1 after unit 6) and 2 respond
15:   days (one after each check). Two lessons a week of about 45 minutes, 32 weeks: the computer science default for
16:   grades 6 to 12 (docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md section 2, Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1), a real elective, so
17:   course days 2w-1 and 2w fall in week w. The 45 minutes hold set-up, clean-up and collecting work: a lesson opens with
18:   a retrieval trace or a predict task of at most five minutes (APPROACH.md, "How a day opens"), device lessons keep
19:   three minutes at the start to open the editor file and four at the end to save the work as a downloaded file and put
20:   devices away, so a device lesson teaches for about 33 minutes and an unplugged one for about 38. Each unit's days
21:   include its end-of-unit assessment and end with a respond day (APPROACH.md, Pacing), with the pilot's one shape for
22:   a unit that a course-level check follows directly: in units 3 and 6 the check stands for the unit's own end-of-unit
23:   assessment and the check's respond day is the unit's respond day (pilot For the lead 2). So units 1 to 6 teach on
24:   11, 10, 7, 7, 10 and 7 days, 52 of the 64. Days follow the load of the rows: units 1 and 2 own the four programming
25:   and design rows, each a device row with a trace, modify and make order and a scored product with its draft,
26:   feedback and revision days, and get 13 and 12; unit 5 owns three ids and begins a fourth, two of them device rows,
27:   and gets 12; units 3, 4 and 6 own one, two and two ids, mostly unplugged, with a contribution row or two each, and
28:   get 7, 9 and 7. 64 sessions for 17 rows is about the progression read's "about two sessions a row" plus the product
29:   days (docs/reviews/progression-computer-science-2026-11-19.md). No unit runs over 25 days, so there are no mid-unit
30:   assessments. The short path (bar C2) is named by the scope and sequence from these days.
31: - **Check weeks.** Grades 9 to 12 have no stagger rule but the finals week, week 32 (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, stagger
32:   rule 4), and this course keeps off the grade 9 core check weeks anyway (ELA 13, 20 and 32; Algebra I 12 to 13, 22
33:   and 32; Biology 9, 16 and 32; World History I 10, 18 to 19 and 32). The mid-year check is course day 33 in week 17,
34:   its respond day course day 34 in the same week; the year-end check is course day 63 in week 32, the finals week, its
35:   respond day course day 64 in the same week. Units that hold no course-level check run across the core weeks.
36:   Overlaps with other after-core subjects' check weeks are the lead's calendar pass (For the lead 1).
37: - **Standard.** The 12 HS ids of the 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards that the progression gives grade 9
38:   (STANDARD.md "Ownership of MS and HS ids"): HS-PRO-VD-16, HS-ALG-PS-01, HS-ALG-PS-02, HS-PRO-PD-12, HS-ALG-PS-04,
139:   personal information parts of unit 5 carry health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word
