  CT7 abstract and find patterns (units 1 to 5), CT8 compare approaches and build (units 1, 2, 4), CT9 test, debug and
  refine (units 1, 2), IC3 explain, give and take feedback (units 1, 2, 3, 5, 6), IC4 run a project with milestones
  (unit 2), IC5 pair roles that switch (units 1, 2), HCD10 include different users (unit 6), HCD12 keep users informed
  (units 4, 5), ESR1 weigh who gains and who is harmed (units 3, 6), ESR2 protect users' data (units 4, 5).
  Dispositions are named in the unit plans, not here.
- **Base text.** None. The partial base (England's archived Teach Computing Curriculum) covers kindergarten to grade 8
  only (STANDARD.md section 2, Cory 2026-11-16), so grade 9 is our own writing: programs, case cards, toy system
  pages, data tables and keys are written by Curio Class for each unit (the "Curio Class writes" line of each unit).
  Real cases follow STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days": told by what happened, two views, a dated free source
  in the teacher source line, government bodies and courts named, never companies. Every invented table, program or
  case is marked "invented for practice" on its teacher page.
- **Language and editor.** Python, run in Curio Class's own browser editor (STANDARD.md "Our own editor" and "Blocks to
  text": grade 8 on is Python), with a text view, a step-through trace and stated tests that show "yes" or "not yet".
  A program students read is at most twenty lines (APPROACH.md, Reading load). Grade 9 uses lists, key-value records
  (Python dictionaries, always called key-value records in student copy so they are not confused with a data
  dictionary), procedures with parameters, loops, selection, the editor's built-in random and math libraries and one
  small class (an object with data and actions); inheritance, recursion and advanced structures are out.
- **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") defines them, in computer science's words (APPROACH.md):
  Understanding is an unplugged or trace lesson, the first teaching of a construct or idea (a list drawn as a
  list-diagram and traced, an operating system acted out on cards, a predict task on a given program). Fluency is a
  modify lesson: change a working program or design in small, checked steps (unit 1 lessons 2, 4 and 9, unit 2
  lessons 3 and 5, unit 3 lesson 5, unit 4 lesson 5), plus the three-minute retrieval traces; grade 9 has a modest
  share of it. Application is a make, design or society lesson: build a program or a design against criteria, write
  a data dictionary, audit a case, choose a device with evidence, or draft and revise a short analysis (named in each
  unit). Check is the end-of-unit assessment or a course-level check; Respond acts on it by error letter. Inside a unit:
  understanding before modify before make, and no make day before a trace or modify day on the same construct.
- **Evidence.** Daily evidence follows APPROACH.md "The form of daily evidence": an exit ticket with a short program
  and "what happens?" on trace days, the program with its look-fors and a "what I tested and what I changed" line on
  modify and make days, a claim with two views and dated sources on society days. Every end-of-unit assessment is a
  sitting with unseen items, never the unit's own anchor: read and trace items, a short build or debug task (on the
  device or on paper), and, in a society unit, a short audit or claim task (APPROACH.md, unit field 5). Units 1, 2 and
  6 also score a product (a design, a modular program, a short analysis) by rubric, whose draft, written feedback and
  revision days sit before the end-of-unit day and are counted in the unit. Each owned id has its own look-for. No score
  comes from a lesson that first teaches a row. Units 3 and 6 are assessed in the course-level check that follows them.
  The respond day reteaches by error letter and repeats the missed item in a new form.
- **Devices.** One device per pair is the default on a device day, with driver and navigator roles that switch at a
  stated time (APPROACH.md, section field 7). Every device day is written four ways (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"):
  one device per pair, a few devices at stations with the other pairs on the paper version, one screen with the class
  predicting and voting before each run, or no device. Three owned ids are met only on a device (STANDARD.md "Devices
  and rooms"): HS-DAT-DC-21 (unit 4), HS-PRO-PD-12 (unit 2) and HS-SYS-HW-30 (unit 5). On the no-device path those
  days teach the idea on paper (dice and cards generate a small simulated set; the module split is done on paper with
  the solution key; a paper simulator is stepped through), and the course record marks each "part met without a
  device"; the course never says the id was met. Every other owned id is met in full on paper: programs are written on
  paper, traced with a trace-table and checked by a partner against the stated tests.
- **Paper path.** Every lesson has its paper version: printed programs with trace-tables, list-diagrams and named-box
  sheets, card sets for the unplugged lessons, the toy system pages printed as step-through sheets. No tool asks for a
  student account, stores a student's name or sends anything off the device; work is saved as a file the student
  downloads or printed (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety").
- **Reading level and vocabulary.** Grade 9 students read grade-level text; teacher pages explain each idea at adult
  level for a teacher not trained in the subject (bar item 9). Each unit names five to eight words taught in depth,
  with the everyday words that have a computing meaning taught as two meanings (record, key, library, object, driver,
  seed); assignment is read "x becomes x plus 1", never "x equals x plus 1" (lens). Sentence frames: "I predict ___
  because line ___ says ___" and "This structure fits because ___". A student may answer a trace in numbers, arrows
  or the home language.
- **Swap or leave out.** STANDARD.md section 3 lets a school swap or leave out a named unit for its community's
  reasons. Unit 6's career lesson (begun HS-SOC-CE-46, owned in grade 12) may be left out with no id lost; unit 6's
  history case may be swapped for another computing technology's trajectory with two secondary sources, so
  HS-SOC-HI-38 and HS-SOC-ET-40 are always claimed (bar part 5 fails a course with nothing on society). Unit 4's
  large-scale collection cases may be swapped for other cases; the unit is not left out (it holds both DAT ids).
  Units 1 to 3 and 5 carry the programming, algorithm and systems ids the later grades build on and are not swapped
  or left out.
- **Safety.** No lab or field work, so no laboratory or field safety guide applies. Devices: cords behind the
  table, two hands to carry, a stated break from the screen in any lesson with more than 25 minutes at the device.
  The optional microcontroller board in unit 5 runs on low voltage from a USB cable, with no mains power, no soldering
  and no battery packs opened; the simulated device is the default. Security lessons use only the toy systems built
  into the page: no scanning, no attack and no test of a real school or home system (STANDARD.md "Privacy and
  safety"). The scope and sequence copies these lines into each device and security day.
- **Costs.** Provisional, for a class of 30, beyond the school's own devices: unit 1 $6 (printing trace-tables,
  list-diagram and named-box sheets, the record cards), unit 2 $5 (printing the long program and the module cards,
  rubric sheets), unit 3 $6 (two dice per pair, about 30 dice at $3, printing the task and case cards), unit 4 $4
  (printing the survey tables and dictionary sheets), unit 5 $6 (printing the operating system card set, network
  diagram and measure cards; the optional board about $25 a board, one per class for a teacher demonstration, not
  counted), unit 6 $4 (printing the timeline strips and the two-source packets), about $31 in all.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
  no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units 3 and 4 touch bias and data about people. Unit 3's audit
  cases (invented for practice) touch students who live far from school, students with disabilities and families with
  less money; before it reads, the agent studies NIST Special Publication 1270, "Towards a Standard for Identifying
  and Managing Bias in Artificial Intelligence" (2022, nist.gov, free) as background, and the brief's own community
  sources, two of them owned by the communities the cases touch, and records them. Unit 4's large-scale collection
  cases (a national census, a store loyalty program, told without names) touch families who may fear government data
  collection, including immigrant families: the agent checks no task asks about a student's or family's status, home
  or data, and that the census case gives the agency's own published privacy rules beside a critical view. No case in
  grade 9 is about a Native nation's data (data sovereignty is grade 11's, g11-data-scale-ethics). Unit 6's history
  case includes a civilian airliner shot down in 1983 with everyone aboard killed: the agent checks it is told as a
  dated fact with no images and no role play. Unit 6's career lesson is private by default. The online safety and
  personal information parts of unit 5 carry health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word
  (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md; APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground). The course's checks get the answer-key agent's
  read; no unit is hands-on in the laboratory sense, so no safety agent read is planned beyond the Safety lines above,
  which the sensitivity read confirms are on each device day.

| 1 | Systems that hold up. *How do we keep a school's systems working, safe and usable for everyone who needs them?* Anchor: a map of an invented school district's network on the board, one link cut, and a fake "reset your password" message on the screen beside it | 13 | g8-network-failure; g8-cyber-attacks; g8-choose-a-system; g8-design-for-all | MS-SYS-SE-33; MS-SYS-IM-36 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: CT6 (state each user's need as a test condition); CT9 and HCD11 (test a design change, fix, test again); HCD10 (who a system leaves out); IC5 (team roles on the design days); ESR2 (protect users' accounts and data) | Day 1 (unplugged): hook and question; retrieval of g7-internet-resilience (find another path); a new map and a new failure (a cut link, a down server) traced, and the packets' new path redrawn on the packet-strip (~MS-SYS-NT-35, used in a new setting, owned in grade 7). Day 2 (application): redraw the whole map and add one backup link so the failure cuts no one off; justify where. Day 3 (unplugged): retrieval of the triangle (g7-cia-effects); common attacks on cards (a fake message asking for a password, a program hiding inside another, a guessed password, a flood of requests that takes a service down) matched to the side they hurt; the cybersecurity word page. Day 4 (device, toy inbox built into the page): sort messages into safe and fake by named clues; each attack matched to its prevention (updates, backups, a long passphrase, a second check, asking a trusted adult). Day 5 (application): a protection plan for an invented school system, three attacks, each with its side, its prevention and why that prevention fits (MS-SYS-SE-33, evaluate both attacks and preventions). Day 6 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-system-requirements; two systems for a school office compared on the system-diagram against requirements another group wrote, with an energy note and an ethics note. Day 7: a written justification checked line by line against those requirements by a partner (~MS-SYS-HW-30, used in a new setting, owned in grade 6). Day 8 (device, practice sign-up page): retrieval of g7-access-differences; the page tried against four users' stated needs as test conditions (keyboard only, text enlarged to twice its size, read without color, plain words for a reader new to English); the barrier found. Day 9 (team design): each team proposes a design change for the barrier on the system-diagram and a paper mock-up. Day 10: teammates test the change against each stated need and record what still fails. Day 11: revise and retest (MS-SYS-IM-36, improve a design together for different users). Out: no real attack, scan or account, no encryption mechanics (grade 9's g9-security-measures compares measures), no protocol detail (grade 9 and 10), no operating system layer (g9-operating-systems). End-of-unit (day 12): a new map with a new failure to redraw with a backup link; three new attacks, each matched to its side and a prevention and the choice explained (SE-33); a new system with a new barrier: a design change for two stated needs and how the team would test it (IM-36); a short justification against new requirements, recorded for the HW-30 thread; respond day 13. Tools: a toy inbox page (messages built into the page, job: practice spotting a fake message with no real account); a practice sign-up page (job: a barrier to find and a change to test, keyboard and zoom testable). Curio Class writes: the network maps, attack and prevention cards, the word page, the requirements sets, the sign-up page and its printed version, the user-need cards | First because it sets the year's rules for shared devices, accounts and toy security pages before the project and the AI days use them, and because its four rows need only grade 7's systems rows, which open as retrieval. The barrier-and-test days teach testing with users' stated needs, which unit 2's refine-for-users days use on the students' own programs. Base: archived Year 9 "Introduction of cybersecurity" lessons 2, 4 and 6 for days 3 to 5 (Source map); the network, system choice and design days are our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's social engineering lesson order (how the trick works, then the clue to spot it) inside days 3 and 4, zero added days | Disability: needs are test conditions on cards, never a role a student acts out; talk is about what the page lets a person do, never about a classmate; use the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Online safety (days 3 to 5): no real password, account or message is used or asked for; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word, since a fake-message day can bring up a real one a student or family received. The flood attack is described, never run. Devices: cords behind the table, carried closed. Agent reads: sensitivity (the unit 1 pack in Sensitivity reads first). Facts: the fake-message clues are checked against the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency's "Recognize and Report Phishing" page (cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing, as of 2026-11-25); the district and its network are invented for practice. |
| 2 | Make it, credit it, hand it over. *What does it take to make a program someone else can use, trust and build on?* Anchor: a working word-guess game from "another class" run on the screen: it accepts a guess of the wrong length, shows right letters only in green, and uses a word-picking function with no credit | 15 | g8-text-project; g8-attribution; g8-refine-for-users | MS-PRO-PD-14; MS-PRO-TR-20 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT8 (design with a flowchart and pseudocode, then build); CT7 (a procedure reused for every guess); CT9 (planned test-table, one fix at a time); IC4 (project milestones on a shared plan); IC5 (driver and navigator, switched at the halfway signal); IC3 (a partner reads and explains the code); ESR2 (credit others' code); HCD11 (refine for use and access) | Day 14: hook and question; retrieval of g7-design-with-variables (a flowchart read back). Day 15 (trace, device or paper): the worked word-guess game (about twenty lines of Python: a list of words, a list of letters guessed, a tries-left variable, a procedure that checks one guess) traced with a trace-table and a list-diagram; retrieval of g7-lists and g7-analyze-code (the role of each line). Day 16 (modify): add words to the list and change the loop that shows the hidden word with blanks; trace again. Day 17 (modify): change the procedure so a guess of the wrong length is refused with a plain message, and the tries rule; recheck the old tests. Day 18 (design, unplugged): each pair designs its own small text game on a word list from a neutral topic (planets, US state capitals, animals, a class-library book) as a flowchart and pseudocode, with its test-table planned before any code; a pair may instead design a number-guess game on the same plan. Days 19 and 20 (make): build in text, list, loop, procedure and score, tests run each day (~MS-ALG-PS-01 and ~MS-PRO-RD-17, used in a new setting, owned in grade 7). Day 21 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-reference-docs; cases of copying a function, remixing a project and using an image, sorted by when credit and when permission are needed; licenses in plain words (free to use with credit, free to use with no conditions, all rights kept); why credit matters to the person who made it and to the next reader. Day 22 (device): the project adds a word-picking function from a supplied page of another person's code with a stated license; students write its credit comment and a two-sentence justification of why credit and permission matter in software (MS-PRO-PD-14). Day 23 (make, draft day): finish the draft, run every test. Day 24: hand-over: retrieval of g7-collab-text's code review checklist; a partner traces the key part on a trace-table and writes what it does, one thing unclear and one question. Day 25 (understanding then test): retrieval of g7-test-refine; the anchor game tried by keyboard alone, enlarged, without color and with its output read aloud line by line, its faults named as a worked example; then two classmates try each project the same way and write usability and accessibility feedback on a form. Day 26 (refine): one change at a time from the feedback, each logged. Day 27: retest with the same two classmates; the change log finished (MS-PRO-TR-20). Day 28: hand-in: final test-table run, rubric self-check, one paragraph on what was tested and changed; scored on the project rubric. Out: no key-value record or structure choice (grade 9's g9-structures-program), no parameters design beyond one procedure (grade 9's g9-optimize-procedures), no modules or libraries (g9-modular), no timed guesses, no student names stored. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 29) stands for it, with the project rubric score from day 28. Tools: our own editor (Python text, a step-through trace, stated tests shown as "yes" or "not yet"; job: build, trace and test the game; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md); a supplied code page with a license line. Curio Class writes: the worked game with its trace and bugs, the topic word lists, the word-picking code page and its license line, the credit cases, the feedback form, the rubric, a finished solution for every step | After unit 1, whose stated-needs testing these refine days reuse on students' own programs, and the first programming unit of the year so the project's list, loop and procedure are fresh for unit 4's code samples, which are written against the same game. Attribution sits inside the build, on the day a borrowed function enters, so the credit line is written for a real use. Refine-for-users follows the draft so feedback has a program to act on. Base: archived Year 9 "Python programming with sequences of data" lessons 1 and 5 for days 15, 19, 20 and 23, and Year 8 "Layers of computing systems" lesson 6 (sharing and open source) for day 21 (Source map); grade 7 adapted the sequences unit's lessons 2 to 4 | Candidate: kept, the partial base's live-coding of the worked program before pairs try it, inside day 15, zero added days | Word lists come from neutral topics; no word or prompt asks about a student, a family or a home. No student's name, photo or voice goes into a program (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety"). Feedback is about the program, never the classmate: the form asks what worked, one barrier met and one suggestion. Devices: cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 3 | Data that answers back. *Does a hotter day bring more swimmers to the town pool, and how far can a dataset's answer be trusted?* Anchor: sixty days of an invented town pool's daily high temperature and visitor count, and a headline that claims "heat doubles pool crowds" | 12 | g8-relationships; g8-investigation-summary; g8-biased-decisions | MS-DAT-DI-25; MS-DAT-DI-27; MS-DAT-IM-29 | Concept area: data and analysis. Practices: CT6 (pose the investigation's question); CT7 (find the pattern in the scatter); IC3 (written summary, partner feedback); ESR1 (whose decisions bias a dataset, and who is hurt by the result) | Day 31 (unplugged): hook and question; retrieval of g7-sort-filter-groups and g7-viz-choices; twelve days of the table plotted by hand as a scatter plot. Day 32 (device): the whole table in the table-and-scatter tool; the trend described and used to predict visitors on a 31 degree day, and days classified as busy or quiet by a threshold (MS-DAT-DI-25, find a relationship with a tool and use it to predict and to classify). Day 33 (device): two more invented sets, one with no relationship and one where one value falls as the other rises; which variable helps predict, and that a relationship is not a cause. Days 34 and 35 (investigation, device): pairs choose a question of one of three supplied sets (the pool set, a plant-growth-and-light set, a bus-ridership-and-rain set, all invented for practice), find a relationship, make a prediction, and draw the fairest chart (retrieval of g7-viz-choices). Day 36 (understanding): a given summary with an overreaching claim graded against its evidence; the parts of a summary named: question, method, data source, result, limits, possible biases. Day 37 (draft): each student writes the summary of the pair's investigation. Day 38: written partner feedback on a checklist, then revision (MS-DAT-DI-27). Day 39 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-personal-data; one invented dataset (a town's survey about a new park) followed through its stages, collect, clean, label, summarize and train, with a decision at each stage that can bias it (who was asked, which rows were dropped, how a label was chosen). Day 40 (application): each pair changes one stage's decision and redraws the chart, or reruns a toy model, to show how the decision carries into a misleading conclusion or a poor model (MS-DAT-IM-29). Out: no line of best fit by equation or correlation coefficient (math grade 8 fits a line by eye; high school statistics), no simulated data (grade 9's g9-simulate-data), no data dictionary (g9-data-dictionary), no real person's data. End-of-unit (day 41): a new table: find the relationship with the tool (or by hand on the paper path) and make one prediction and one classification (DI-25); a new summary with two faults: name the missing limit and the claim the evidence does not support (DI-27), with day 38's revised summary scored on its checklist; a new dataset's stages: place two decisions that would bias it and say what each does to the result (IM-29); respond day 42. Tools: a table-and-scatter tool (a table, a scatter, a trend line drawn by eye, a filter; job: find and use the relationship; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the four invented data sets with "invented for practice" on each, the overreaching summary, the stage cards for the park survey, the summary checklist | After math grade 8 teaches scatter plots and association (see Cross-subject), so the tool days use a scatter the students already read. Before unit 4 because its biased-decisions row ends in a "poor model", which unit 4's classifier then tests, and because a model's test results are a data table read with this unit's moves. The summary follows the investigation so it summarizes a process the student ran. Base: archived Year 9 "Data science" lessons 1 to 6 (Source map), its global data sets replaced by invented ones | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "clean it up" step as the clean stage of day 39, zero added days | All data is invented and about things (temperature, visitors, plants, buses); the park survey's people are invented and unnamed. No class data about students, health or homes. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
