word); the teacher runs it on one screen with a school-approved tool and no student accounts, or opens Curio Class's saved outputs; the class judges the output against the problem and says what a person must still check (MS-ALG-PS-05). Day 50 (trace): a saved generated sample for a requirement from the unit 2 game (keep a list of past scores and show the best) traced with a test-table; the first wrong line named as a worked example. Day 51 (device): four saved samples for the same requirement, some right and some flawed (one calls a function that does not exist); each run on the test-table and judged for accuracy and usability, first wrong line named. Day 52 (application): a written verdict on which sample a pair would put into its game project, and one change that makes it clearer (MS-PRO-RD-18, judge generated code for a programming project). Out: no training a model (grade 7 trained one), no AI algorithm types (grade 9's g9-ai-tour), no probability of outputs (grade 9's g9-det-vs-prob), no live generated-text tool used by a student, no account. End-of-unit (day 53): a new test-results table and model card: two features, one limit, one place it should not be used (ML-08); a new rule with a harm: modify it and show the retest (IM-11); a new saved output judged against its problem (PS-05); a new generated sample: first wrong line, a test that catches it, and the fix (RD-18); respond day 54. Tools: a classifier test page (a model built into the page, its test set and results; job: read accuracy and errors by kind; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md); our own editor (job: run the saved samples on the test-table); on day 49 only, a school-approved tool on the teacher's screen or the saved outputs. Curio Class writes: the fruit drawings and test set, the model card, the lottery cases, the prompt task and the saved outputs, the four code samples with a key naming each flaw | After unit 3, whose biased-decision stages explain why a model fails on some examples, and after math grade 8's function unit (see Cross-subject). After unit 2, so the generated code samples are judged against the students' own game project, which is what MS-PRO-RD-18 asks. Model testing comes before the generated-output days so students meet "a computer's output can be wrong in patterned ways" on a model they can see before they judge a tool's code. Our own (the partial base's key stage 3 has no AI unit) | Candidate: kept, a second lottery rule with a different harm as the respond day's new form, zero added days | Fruit drawings, never faces or people, so no model sorts people. The lottery's cases are invented; talk is about the rule, never about which families have cars or time. Day 49: no student types into or makes an account with a tool (STANDARD.md "Generated-text tools"); a school with no approved tool uses the saved outputs and the course marks MS-ALG-PS-05 part met. No page says how Curio Class was made (STANDARD.md "The word AI"). Facts: the model card's parts follow Mitchell and others, "Model Cards for Model Reporting", 2019 (arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993), teacher background only; the model and its results are invented for practice. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
