ned (opening, evidence, rebuttal, summary). Day 59: the structured debate on the social, cultural and environmental effects (water, power, jobs, land, the town's character), every claim backed, rebuttals on the other side's evidence (MS-SOC-ET-42, debate effects on a local community; ~MS-SOC-HU-43, used in a new setting, owned in grade 6). Day 60: each student writes their own claim, its backing and a rebuttal to the strongest point against it. Day 61 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-automation-work; each student sketches one computing career path of their own interest, its skills, its training and how automation may change it, shared at the level of ideas (~MS-SOC-CE-44, used in a new setting, owned in grade 6). Day 62: the year's look-back: each student places one idea from each unit on a one-page map of the five concept areas and writes the question they would take to grade 9. Out: no company or product named in student or teacher copy, no vote that settles a real town question, no policy writing (high school), no technical comparison with the established technology (grade 9's g9-emerging-differences). End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 63) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the two saved transcripts, the route tool case, the credit records case card, the debate packet with two sides and their sources, the career sketch page | Last because its rows gather the year: unit 1's systems, unit 3's data and unit 4's models are the evidence a debate on a new technology needs, and the history case lends the debate its question of what a rule can do. Debate follows ELA grade 8's discussion unit (see Cross-subject). The career sketch closes the middle school career thread and hands it to grade 9. Our own (the partial base has no society unit) | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a town planner; the packet carries both sides and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | Video visits (day 55): the case is about how a service was designed and who could use it, never about a student's or family's health, internet or devices; no student says what their home has; use the wording the sensitivity read settles. Debate (days 58 to 60): two sides, neither declared right; students may argue the side they did not choose, and a student whose family works in power, water or technology is not asked about it. Credit records (day 57): about the rule and the records, never a family's debts or credit. Career sketch (day 61): no question about a family member's job; invented, public and trusted-adult options as in career education's rule (Desk q-fix-career-1); automation can touch a family's work, so the teacher note says so (APPROACH.md, "Work and automation"). Facts: the video visit figures come from the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation's 2022 report on national telehealth use in 2021 (aspe.hhs.gov, as of its publication date), checked at source when the day is written; the credit records case rests on the Federal Trade Commission's page on the Fair Credit Reporting Act (ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act); the data center figures come from Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory's "2024 United States Data Center Energy Usage Report" for the Department of Energy (December 2024, as of that date). Agent reads: sensitivity (the unit 5 sources in Sensitivity reads first). |

